CAT = "serLicensing"; SEG = "LIC"
Q = [
("What is the minimum age to apply for a London PHV driver's licence?",
 "21", ["18", "23", "25"],
 "You must be 21 or older when you apply, and there is no upper age limit. 18 is the age for a car driving licence, not a London PHV driver's licence."),
("How long must you have held a full driving licence before TfL will license you as a PHV driver?",
 "At least three years", ["At least eighteen months", "At least two years", "At least five years"],
 "The handbook requires a full DVLA, Northern Ireland, EU or EEA licence that is at least three years old."),
("Which medical standards must a London PHV driver meet?",
 "DVLA Group 2 standards", ["DVLA Group 1 standards", "NHS fitness standards", "TfL's own eyesight rules"],
 "PHV drivers must meet the DVLA Group 2 medical standards, the higher standard used for lorry and bus drivers. Group 1 is the standard for ordinary car drivers."),
("What does the Topographical Skills Assessment test?",
 "Route planning and map reading", ["Equality law and safeguarding", "English speaking and listening", "Daily vehicle safety checks"],
 "The topographical test checks your ability to select and plan a route, read a map and identify locations. Equality and safeguarding are covered by the SERU assessment."),
("Which level of English must a London PHV driver prove?",
 "B1 on the CEFR scale", ["A2 on the CEFR scale", "B2 on the CEFR scale", "C1 on the CEFR scale"],
 "Applicants must show at least B1 English on the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages."),
("How does TfL assess your English reading and writing for a PHV licence?",
 "Through the SERU assessment", ["Through a separate essay test", "Through the topographical test", "Through an interview at TfL"],
 "Passing SERU also shows the reading and writing skills TfL needs. Speaking and listening are tested separately in a TfL building."),
("How long does a London PHV driver's licence normally last?",
 "Three years", ["One year", "Five years", "Eighteen months"],
 "A PHV driver's licence normally lasts three years; if TfL issues a shorter one, the covering letter explains why. It is the PHV vehicle licence that lasts one year."),
("A TfL Authorised Officer asks to see your PHV driver's licence. What must you do?",
 "Show it then or within seven days", ["Show it at a TfL office within 21 days", "Post a copy to your operator", "Show your DVLA licence instead"],
 "You must let a police officer or TfL Authorised Officer see your licence there and then or within a maximum of seven days."),
("Where should you keep your PHV driver's licence?",
 "At home, with a copy to your operator", ["In the glovebox, in case of checks", "Displayed on the dashboard while working", "Handed to your operator to keep"],
 "Keep the licence safe at home and give a copy to every licensed operator you work with. Operators must keep copies of your PHV and driving licences."),
("When must you wear your PHV driver's badge?",
 "Whenever you are working as a driver", ["Only when a passenger is on board", "When a TfL officer asks to see it", "At airports and late-night venues"],
 "You must wear the badge whenever you are working, including while waiting for a booking or travelling to a pick-up, and it must be clearly visible."),
("Which details appear on a PHV driver's badge?",
 "Name, photo, licence number, end date", ["Name, address and date of birth", "Photo, home address and operator", "Name, vehicle registration and operator"],
 "The badge shows your name, photograph, licence number and the date your licence ends. Passengers can check these against the details the operator sends them."),
("Why does the PHV driver's badge include Braille?",
 "So blind passengers can check it", ["So TfL officers can scan it quickly", "So it is harder to copy", "So operators can record it"],
 "The Braille text lets a vision-impaired passenger know that the driver is licensed."),
("A passenger asks for your licence number. What should you do?",
 "Let them see your badge", ["Refer them to your operator", "Tell them it is confidential", "Show them your DVLA licence"],
 "The handbook says to let the passenger see your badge so they can write the number down."),
("TfL has given you an exemption from wearing your badge. What must you do?",
 "Carry the notice whenever you work", ["Display the notice on the windscreen", "Tell each passenger before the trip", "Wear the badge only at night"],
 "A badge exemption comes with an exemption notice that you must carry at all times when working. Notices for wheelchair or assistance dog exemptions are the ones shown in the windscreen."),
("Your PHV driver's badge is stolen. Who should you report it to?",
 "TfL and the police", ["Only your operator", "Only the DVLA", "Only the police"],
 "Report a lost or stolen badge to TfL immediately, and if it was stolen also report it to the police and get a crime reference number. You cannot work without your badge."),
("TfL has replaced your lost badge and you then find the original. What must you do?",
 "Return it to TfL", ["Keep it as a spare", "Destroy it safely", "Give it to your operator"],
 "If you find the original badge or licence after it has been replaced, you must return it to TfL."),
("Who owns your PHV driver's badge and licence?",
 "TfL, at all times", ["You, once issued", "Your PHV operator", "The DVLA"],
 "Badges and licences remain TfL property at all times, even after they expire, and must be returned when they expire or you are no longer licensed."),
("Which of these may exempt you from supplying a PHV medical form?",
 "A current London taxi driver's licence", ["A full car licence held for over ten years", "A letter from your PHV operator", "A recent eye test certificate"],
 "You may be exempt if you hold a full or provisional DVLA Group 2 licence (issued after January 1997), a current London taxi driver's licence or a valid JAA pilot's licence. A car licence is only Group 1."),
("From what age must PHV drivers have a medical examination every time they renew?",
 "45", ["40", "50", "60"],
 "From 45, a medical is needed at each licence renewal. From 65 it becomes every year."),
("From what age must PHV drivers have a medical assessment every year?",
 "65", ["60", "70", "75"],
 "Annual medical assessments start at 65. Between 45 and 65 the medical is due at each renewal."),
("Between medicals you are diagnosed with epilepsy. What should you do?",
 "Tell TfL immediately", ["Wait for your next medical", "Tell only your operator", "Tell TfL at your renewal"],
 "You should tell TfL immediately about any new condition that may affect your driving. Epilepsy is one of the examples the handbook lists."),
("Which of these is listed as a condition you should tell TfL about?",
 "High blood pressure", ["Mild seasonal hay fever", "A sprained wrist", "A short-term cold"],
 "The handbook's examples include diabetes, epilepsy, eye conditions, heart conditions, high blood pressure, neurological conditions, some medication and psychiatric illness."),
("How soon should you tell TfL that you have moved home?",
 "Within 21 days", ["Within 7 days", "Within 72 hours", "At your next renewal"],
 "Tell TfL about a new home address within 21 days, and also keep your email and phone details up to date."),
("Apart from TfL, who must you tell by law when you change your address?",
 "The DVLA", ["The Home Office", "The local council", "The Metropolitan Police"],
 "The law requires you to tell the DVLA, which issues a new driving licence. The address on your PHV licence should match your DVLA licence."),
("You are disqualified from driving. What must you do as well as telling TfL?",
 "Return your PHV licence and badge", ["Keep working until the appeal is heard", "Ask your operator for a permit", "Apply to TfL for a new badge"],
 "A disqualification must be reported immediately, and you must also return your London PHV driver's licence and badge to TfL."),
("When must you tell TfL that you have been arrested?",
 "Immediately", ["Only if you are charged", "Only if you are convicted", "At your next renewal"],
 "You must tell TfL immediately if you are arrested, charged, convicted or cautioned for any crime. Waiting for a charge or conviction is too late."),
("You get penalty points for speeding. Who is responsible for telling TfL?",
 "You are", ["The police", "Your operator", "The DVLA"],
 "You are responsible for reporting offences, including fixed penalties with points. The handbook says not to rely on the police informing TfL."),
("What does the handbook say about parking tickets that carry no penalty points?",
 "TfL may act if you get them often", ["You must report each one at once", "You must report them within 21 days", "They can never affect your licence"],
 "You need not report PCNs or parking tickets without points, but TfL may take licensing action if it learns you get them frequently."),
("Your PHV driver's licence is revoked. How long do you have to return it?",
 "Seven days", ["21 days", "72 hours", "Fourteen days"],
 "A revoked licence must be returned within seven days. If it is not, or the revocation has immediate effect, TfL may send an Authorised Officer to collect it."),
("TfL writes asking for your comments on a complaint about you. What should you do?",
 "Respond with your comments", ["Ignore it unless police call", "Ask your operator to answer", "Wait for a formal hearing"],
 "TfL can only decide on the information it receives, so it is important to respond to its request yourself."),
("What does an enhanced DBS check show?",
 "Convictions, cautions and warnings", ["Driving test results and points", "Credit history and unpaid debts", "Medical history and past prescriptions"],
 "An enhanced DBS check shows convictions, cautions, reprimands and final warnings, relevant local police information and whether you are on a barred list."),
("How must a PHV licence applicant obtain the DBS certificate?",
 "Through TfL's chosen company", ["From any DBS provider you choose", "From their local police", "From their PHV operator"],
 "TfL uses a separate company to arrange DBS checks, and applicants must use that company."),
]
